 more  reference points  um  i'm sure that  you know seniors  of my age  that their memory and and some of their  you know  neural skills have declined just like  their body is not as strong  uh but i i don't have the impression  personally that it impacts on my ability  to learn languages  i don't think language learning is so  much a matter of memory as i've said  before i think it's although memory  certainly plays a part i think it's more  a matter of noticing  you know if i'm listening to check i  have to notice how it's pronounced i  have to notice  where you know the  emphasis  is in a word  i have to notice certain structures i  try to make them natural i try to  remember certain things but i can't  remember what i can't notice and so i i  think noticing is is the big thing  and i think noticing it depends on a  number of factors  uh it depends on our state of mind uh  are we interested do we think we can do  it are we alert are we you know keen  uh we're more likely to notice  if we are um i'm an experienced language  learner so i will notice certain things  if there are certain things in check  that are similar to russian i will  notice them  you know if if um  you know some tense things that uh i  mean i noticed for example the  similarity in all european languages in  terms of the structure of the verbs  these are things that i noticed because  i have learned so many languages  i think you notice if you expose  yourself to a lot of the language  through repetitive listening and and  eventually not so repetitive and and  reading and and so more and more things  become clear so that you're able to  notice these other things that you can't  notice at first  uh  i don't think any of these things relate  so much to age uh i mean i would be  quite willing to  to uh now i'm not a good example because  i'm an experienced language learner i've  learned a lot of languages but i don't  feel i feel that i'm a better language  learner today than i was when i was in  