ng  guy  and i can't see why they would have  trouble and i look at my  son mark who uh has become somewhat  belatedly interested in languages he  wasn't that interested when we i was  trying to force him to learn french as a  child  but he learns very well  he's you know i would have thought him  old when i was studying  chinese i think a part of it is the way  i think a lot of the people who are past  20 they think they're not going to be  able to do it  they  are inhibited  they're easily embarrassed  they don't focus on noticing the  language because they're too much  concerned about themselves and worrying  about themselves and can i do it and how  do i sound and  and um  yeah i think i think if you approach  language learning as some kind of a  memory task or you're going to have to  remember a bunch of rules and a bunch of  vocabulary you can quickly persuade  yourself that you can't do it  if you approach language learning it's  something that you're going to basically  immerse yourself in through a lot of  listening and reading or if you're lucky  enough to live in the country where the  language is spoken you can get exposure  and if you feel that you're just going  to be able to learn it and  if you take the initiative of regularly  consulting a grammar book and here again  you talk about noticing  when i start noticing certain structures  and i wonder why then i'm quite keen to  go to the grammar book and look it up  and see some of the rules and the  patterns and and there it has a chance  of sticking whereas if it's kind of  forced on me according to some  curriculum today we're going to study  the subjunctive and i say like i don't  even know what the subjunctive is i  haven't even noticed it yet then it's  not going to stick but if i start  noticing it and why does the verb change  there then i'm ripe  and ready  to to learn that so  to me the big thing is  believing in yourself  liking the task liking what you're doing  um  enjoying it like i mean i